      <title>Fifteen elephant deaths at Kenya park may be due to cyanide poisoning</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/jul/30/elephant-deaths-kenya-park-cyanide-poisoning-wildlife</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Analysis of samples collected from carcasses found traces of toxic chemical compound, says Kenya Wildlife Service&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The recent deaths of 15 elephants near a park in southern Kenya in the past month were likely caused by the animals ingesting pesticides used on nearby tomato farms, the Kenya Wildlife Service (KWS) has said.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Erustus Kanga, the agency’s director general, told Citizen TV that analysis of samples collected from the carcasses had found traces of cyanide, a chemical compound used in farming as a predator control poison and a pest fumigant, which has &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2015/oct/26/22-more-elephants-poisoned-cyanide-zimbabwe-reserve"&gt;also been used by poachers.&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2026/jul/30/elephant-deaths-kenya-park-cyanide-poisoning-wildlife"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
