<item>
      <title>Fifty-three days and counting: 93-year-old leader’s record absence fuels health speculation</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jul/30/record-absence-paul-biya-cameroon-president-health-speculation</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Cameroon’s Paul Biya has not made a statement or been seen since leaving for ‘brief private stay’ at Swiss hotel on 7 June&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A record 53-day absence by the world’s oldest head of state has renewed speculation in his native Cameroon about whether he is dead or alive, even though &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/11/cameroon-bans-discussing-presidents-health-amid-speculation-on-his-absence"&gt;discussing his health is illegal in the country&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;On 7 June, 93-year-old Paul Biya left for Switzerland for what officials described as a “brief private stay” alongside his wife, Chantal. Almost two months later, neither the president nor the first lady have addressed the country or been seen publicly.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/jul/30/record-absence-paul-biya-cameroon-president-health-speculation"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
